heck each image's src and alt for 'Getty'. The task asks to 'check if it contains an image related to 'Getty Images'', so I need to return a result indicating yes or no. I'll implement this by: 1. Using the searchbox to search for the article title. 2. Clicking the search result link to navigate to the article page. 3. Using extract_images_from_page_manual to extract images from that page. 4. Iterating through the images to see if any src or alt contains 'Getty'. 5. Returning 'Yes' if found, 'No' if not. I must ensure to use relative URLs for navigation. The search might return a link; I'll click it directly. After navigation, I'll call extract_images_from_page_manual with the page_url being the current path. Then, process the result and return the answer.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Step 1: Search for the article title\n    searchbox = page.get_by_role(\"searchbox\", name=\"Search\")\n    await searchbox.fill(\"One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says\")\n    search_button = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Search\")\n    await search_button.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"load\")\n    \n    # Step 2: Find and click the article link in the search results\n    # Assuming the search results contain a link with the article title\n    article_link = page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says\", exact=False)\n    await article_link.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"load\")\n    \n    # Step 3: Extract images from the article page using extract_images_from_page_manual\n    current_url = page.url\n    # Get relative path from current URL (assuming base URL is http://localhost:8000)\n    from urllib.parse import urlparse\n    parsed_url = urlparse(current_url)\n    page_url = parsed_url.path + (\"?\" + parsed_url.query if parsed_url.query else \"\")\n    images_data = await extract_images_from_page_manual(page, page_url)\n    \n    # Step 4: Check if any image is related to 'Getty Images'\n    contains_getty_image = False\n    for image in images_data['images']:\n        src = image.get('src', '')\n        alt = image.get('alt', '')\n        if src and 'Getty' in src:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n        if alt and 'Getty' in alt:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n    \n    # Step 5: Return the result\n    if contains_getty_image:\n        return \"Yes\"\n    else:\n        return \"No\"\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'TimeoutError': Timeout 16000ms exceeded.\n=========================== logs ===========================\n\"domcontentloaded\" event fired\n============================================================\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nI am currently on the article page titled 'One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says \u2013 Epoch & Drift Benchmark' with URL 'http://localhost:8000/?p=487', as indicated by the accessibility tree. The task is to check if this article contains an image related to 'Getty Images'. I do not need to navigate away from this page because I am already on the target article. I need to extract images from this page and check for 'Getty' in their src or alt attributes. The knowledge base provides several functions for extracting images: extract_images_from_page, extract_images_from_page_manual, extract_page_metadata, and generate_page_preview. extract_images_from_page_manual is designed to avoid navigation timeouts and has been used successfully in previous tasks, so it is reliable. I will use extract_images_from_page_manual with the current page's relative URL to get all images. Then, I will iterate through the images to check if any src or alt contains 'Getty'. The task asks to 'check if it contains an image related to 'Getty Images'', so I need to return a result indicating yes or no. I must return the API response as-is without processing, but the function returns a dictionary with images data. I will extract the images list and check for 'Getty', then return 'Yes' or 'No' accordingly. Since I am already on the correct page, no further navigation is needed.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Extract images from the current article page using extract_images_from_page_manual\n    from urllib.parse import urlparse\n    current_url = page.url\n    parsed_url = urlparse(current_url)\n    page_url = parsed_url.path + (\"?\" + parsed_url.query if parsed_url.query else \"\")\n    images_data = await extract_images_from_page_manual(page, page_url)\n    \n    # Check if any image is related to 'Getty Images'\n    contains_getty_image = False\n    for image in images_data['images']:\n        src = image.get('src', '')\n        alt = image.get('alt', '')\n        if src and 'Getty' in src:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n        if alt and 'Getty' in alt:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n    \n    # Return the result as required\n    if contains_getty_image:\n        return \"Yes\"\n    else:\n        return \"No\"\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'TimeoutError': Page.goto: Timeout 16000ms exceeded.\nCall log:\n  - navigating to \"http://localhost:8000/?p=487\", waiting until \"load\"\n\n</exception>\n</previous_actions>\n\nHere is the current website state:\n<webpage_accessibility_tree>\ndocument: \"One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says \u2013 Epoch & Drift Benchmark\" {\n  link: \"Epoch & Drift Benchmark\", url: \"/\";\n  link: \"\uf015\", url: \"/\";\n  link: \"Sample Page\", url: \"/?page_id=2\";\n  link: \"site mode button\", url: \"\";\n  link: \"\uf002\", url: \"javascript:...\";\n  main {\n    article {\n      link: \"technology\", url: \"/?cat=16\";\n      link: \"December 18, 2025\", url: \"/?p=487\";\n      link: \"admin\", url: \"/?author=1\";\n      link: \"Leave a Comment on One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation